+# Resource spec: orders
+
+The input an agent fills the AI-FILL markers from (lab 01). The contract it implements is
+in `test/labs/README.md`; `pnpm lab 01` checks it.
+
+- **singular / plural:** `order` / `orders`
+- **route:** `/api/v1/orders`
+- **storage:** default (in-memory repository)
+
+## Fields
+
+| field      | type    | constraints           | default | client-settable |
+| ---------- | ------- | --------------------- | ------- | --------------- |
+| `item`     | string  | 1–200 chars, required | —       | yes             |
+| `quantity` | integer | whole number, 1–100   | `1`     | yes             |
+| `status`   | enum    | `open` \| `fulfilled` | `open`  | yes             |
+
+`item` is not trimmed: the contract only bounds its length.
+
+## Rules
+
+Rules that only need the **payload** go in the schema. Rules that need **stored state**
+go in the service.
+
+| #   | rule                                                                                | where                        | error → HTTP               |
+| --- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------- | -------------------------- |
+| O1  | `quantity` is a whole number from 1 to 100 (a string such as `"2"` is not a number) | schema (payload only)        | validation → 400           |
+| O2  | once an order is `fulfilled` (stored status), a PATCH cannot change its `quantity`  | service (needs stored state) | `RuleViolationError` → 422 |
+
+O2 compares against the **stored** status: an `open` order may change its quantity and
+become `fulfilled` in the same PATCH. A PATCH on a fulfilled order that sends the
+**same** quantity it already has is not a change and is allowed.
+
+## Open questions
+
+Not answered by the contract, so **no rule was invented** for them:
+
+- Can a `fulfilled` order go back to `open`? (Today: yes, nothing forbids it. If it can,
+  O2 is bypassable by reopening, changing the quantity and fulfilling again.)
+- Can a `fulfilled` order change its `item`, or be deleted?
+
+## Out of scope
+
+Ownership (orders are not scoped to a user; any authenticated caller sees all of them),
+pricing, persistence beyond memory.
